\startsection[title={Own contributions / workflows I contributed / concrete workflows}]

% (I am the one who can create usable interfaces and/for pipelines and fill in the gaps where “cobblestones” are missing)

\startsubsection[title=Destiny]
% efficient and flexible diffusion maps for R
Destiny is a flexible and fast tool for the creation and visualization of diffusion maps in R.
It can utilize a number of distance metrics to create a distance matrix with, as well as sparse distance matrices, including euclidean, cosine, and rank correlation distance.
I achieved Destiny’s high performance by reusing this sparsity structure using sparse distance matrices with larger distances removed.
To this end, Destiny starts with symmetric kNN matrices and uses the symmetric variant of diffusion maps.
This tactic has later been used by Scanpy and other tools using a kNN search as simplifying preprocessing step.
For Destiny’s stability, I also helped develop the concept of locally scaled diffusion maps as first described in the supplementary materials of \inlinecite[haghverdi2016].
I contributed to and developed a number of independent building blocks for use in Destiny.
This includes a nearest neighbor search operating in the same space the diffusion kernel is applied to.
For Destiny, I contributed to a few projects it depends on to support determinism if a random seed is set.
Destiny also contained a perceptually continuous color-map before they found their way into the major plotting libraries of Python or R.
Apart from diffusion maps, Destiny also supports using its diffusion space to calculate diffusion pseudo time between cells as well as to project new data into the diffusion space of the data used to create the diffusion space.
Destiny supports Bioconductor’s annotated expression matrix classes and directly integrates into the popular plotting library GGplot2.
It contains dedicated plotting routines for diffusion maps and diffusion pseudo time.

\TODO[integrate]
During the parallel development of destiny \cite[angerer2016] and diffusion pseudo time \cite[haghverdi2016], I helped shape the efficient flavor of diffusion maps most used today \TODO[data].
I pioneered the use of a symmetrized kNN graph represented as a symmetric sparse matrix, using this kNN information as a density estimate for the locally scaled kernel $K$ and its sparsity structure for efficient calculation of the symmetric transition probability matrix $T$.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Gene Relevance}]
% combines gene-level with cell-level analysis
The most recent addition to Destiny is the gene relevance algorithm.
Gene relevance is a scoring algorithm designed to combine gene level analysis with cell level analysis.
It allows to identify genes that drive the local positioning of a cell in an embedding.
The initial idea comes from Fabian Theis, while I implemented it and iterated on the design until it was applicable to real data.
I also extended the idea to include scores for defined groups of cells such as clusters or the whole data for a global relevance score per gene.
Destiny includes several plotting routines designed for the different gene relevance concepts.

\TODO[integrate]
I developed and implemented all of the mentioned concepts as part of the destiny R package \cite[angerer2016].
The implementation offers a rich and user friendly API including plotting routines for all concepts.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Scanpy and AnnData}]
% reusable and remix-able workflow building blocks and data structures
Scanpy is a framework for scRNA-Seq preprocessing, analysis, and visualization in Python.
AnnData is a data structure for annotated observation data including scRNA-Seq counts and expressions.
With F. Alexander Wolf I implemented the initial version of both, where he focused on performance while I focused on API and data structure design.
Scanpy is designed around AnnData, storing the results of each processing step in an expression matrix layer or an aligned annotation (see \infig[fig:anndata]).
This data structure enables Scanpy’s flexibility.
It can be persisted to disk in various formats for storage and interoperability with command line tools.
AnnData’s structure allows close interoperability between Scanpy and other tools that use AnnData, including R tools via SingleCellExperiment \cite[lun2017,angerer2019].
For data too large to hold in memory, AnnData supports disk-backed operations.
AnnData’s modular and flexible design philosophy allows Scanpy routines to be effortlessly interspersed with preprocessing and analysis steps of other tools.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={BART-Seq}]
% flexible processing pipeline and Python library
Beyond processing count matrices, I also contributed to a new, targeted RNA-Seq protocol called BART-Seq \cite[uzbas2019].
It is set apart from untargeted single cell and bulk RNA-Seq protocols due to its high sensitivity and low cost.
I designed a demultiplexing and counting strategy for BART-Seq together with Fatma Uzbas and Micha Drucker.
I implemented those strategies as well as visualization routines for the protocol as a Python package.
Part of this package is a pipeline based on Snakemake that performs all steps of the protocol to create count matrices, statistics and visualizations.

